Prove the expansion rule with the real row, and test the fold rule

Point two of this goal was proven with plain boxes — a row that grows keeps its top
edge in a top-anchored list, and rose by exactly the height it gained in the reversed
one that was replaced. What that could not show is the app's own disclosure rows
behaving that way. `RealRowExpansionTest` renders the actual `ReasoningRow` — the
"Think" section a reader taps — inside a real list, clicks it by its own label, and
asserts the row grew while its top edge moved by less than a pixel. That is the case
in question, which the device has never caught: the only growth it has recorded is the
live row's, which is not a row anybody opened.

And the fold rule is now a pure function with tests rather than a condition inside a
composable. `nextFoldedTurns` is two rules: a turn whose rows are on screen is not
folded, whatever the reader is doing, and a reader who is not following keeps whatever
shape the transcript already had. The first is the request in its own words — the rows
being read must not close when their turn ends — and it is why "the reader is at the
tail" is not on its own a reason to fold: at the tail, the turn that has just ended is
exactly the one under the reader's eye as the closing message lands.

337 green.